   "source": [
    "#### Notes / observations\n",
    "\n",
    "Highest coreness is 50 (undirected), but only 5 for in and 6 for out. The dense core exists only when you count both directions together. In one direction alone, no account is in a deeply nested group.\n",
    "\n",
    "There is one connected core at every k. For every k from 2 to 50 the table shows 1 weak component and 0 for the second largest, so the core never splits into separate cores. Most of the structure is also in the core: at k = 11 you still have 3957 of 5648 nodes and 110108 of 117270 edges (94%). The nodes with low coreness are peripheral and have few edges.\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a spike at k = 50. 291 accounts all have coreness 50, far more than at any neighbouring value (13 at k = 49, 96 at k = 47). They have 11062 edges, so about a quarter of all possible pairs among them are connected. That is a very tight block."
   ]
  },
