   "source": [
    "#### Notes / observations\n",
    "\n",
    "We kept the giant weak component and did not apply a k-core (USE_GIANT = True, CORE_K = None). The result is 5424 accounts and 117269 edges.\n",
    "\n",
    "The giant component loses nothing structural. It removes 224 accounts but only 1 edge out of 117270. Those accounts are disconnected from everything else, so they cannot belong to a community or bridge between groups in Parts 2 and 3.\n",
    "\n",
    "A k-core would only remove the periphery. The k-core stays connected for every k (1 component each time), so connectivity gives no reason to filter. Higher k just removes accounts, for example the 10-core loses 28% of the nodes but only 5% of the edges. Those are mostly low-degree accounts retweeting a few hubs, and they are what determines which community a hub belongs to. The density rises with k only because the sparse periphery disappears.\n",
    "\n",
    "The plot shows the structure we expect. A few large hubs sit at the edges and connect into one dense mass of low-degree accounts, with a few small groups attached by thin links. This is the hub-and-spoke pattern from lab 2, so Part 2 should find communities centred on these hubs."
   ]
  },
