      "source": [
        "#### Notes / observations\n",
        "\n",
        "Louvain at res = 1.0 gives the most meaningful structure: 16 communities, no singletons, the highest modularity (0.34), and the largest community holds only 23% of the accounts. Each large hub forms a community with its retweeters, which matches the hub-and-spoke pattern seen earlier, and the dense mass on the right is split into several parts. Louvain at res = 3.0 splits it further (64 communities, modularity 0.27), so it is useful as a finer view, but fragments the core. Infomap (122 communities, 59% in the largest) and Walktrap (328 communities, 238 of them singletons, 78% in the largest) keep one giant community for the dense mass, so they say little about its internal structure. Louvain at res = 0.3 puts 94% of the accounts in one community (modularity 0.03), which is not a useful description. The hubs form their own communities in all methods except Louvain at res = 0.3.\n",
        "\n",
        "Modularity was measured on the undirected network, so it is a fair yardstick for Louvain, but Infomap optimises a different criterion (flow on the directed network), which is why its low modularity does not mean it is wrong by its own measure."
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# load the filtered network from Task 1.3 (directed; vertex name = account ID, weight = number of retweets)\n",
    "g_final = ig.Graph.Read_Pickle('retweet_network_filtered.pickle')\n",
    "# undirected, weighted copy for the algorithms that need it; mutual retweets merge and their weights add up\n",
    "g_und = g_final.as_undirected(mode='collapse', combine_edges={'weight': 'sum'})\n",
    "print('directed  :', g_final.summary())\n",
    "print('undirected:', g_und.summary())\n",
    "\n",
    "# one layout for the filtered network, computed once and reused for every plot\n",
    "LAYOUT_FILE = Path('layout_final.pickle')\n",
    "if LAYOUT_FILE.exists():\n",
    "    layout_final = ig.Layout(pickle.load(open(LAYOUT_FILE, 'rb')))\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    random.seed(1)\n",
    "    layout_final = g_und.layout_drl()\n",
    "    pickle.dump(layout_final.coords, open(LAYOUT_FILE, 'wb'))\n",
    "\n",
    "# community detection\n",
    "partitions, rows = {}, []\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def run(name, detect):\n",
    "    random.seed(42)                                   # reproducible results\n",
    "    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "    cl = detect()\n",
    "    memb = cl.membership\n",
    "    sizes = sorted(Counter(memb).values(), reverse=True)\n",
    "    partitions[name] = memb\n",
    "    rows.append({'structure': name,\n",
    "                 'communities': len(sizes),\n",
    "                 'largest': sizes[0],\n",
    "                 'communities >= 10 nodes': sum(s >= 10 for s in sizes),\n",
    "                 'singletons': sum(s == 1 for s in sizes),\n",
    "                 'modularity': round(g_und.modularity(memb, weights='weight'), 3),   # same yardstick for all\n",
    "                 'time (s)': round(time.perf_counter() - t0, 1)})\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1) modularity-based (Louvain). resolution < 1 -> fewer, larger communities; > 1 -> more, smaller ones\n",
    "for res in (0.3, 1.0, 3.0):\n",
    "    run(f'Louvain (res={res})', lambda res=res: g_und.community_multilevel(weights='weight', resolution=res))\n",
    "# 2) flow-based (Infomap): random walkers on the DIRECTED network, so it uses the retweet direction\n",
    "run('Infomap', lambda: g_final.community_infomap(edge_weights='weight', trials=10))\n",
    "# 3) random-walk-based (Walktrap): short random walks stay inside a community; dendrogram cut at best modularity\n",
    "run('Walktrap', lambda: g_und.community_walktrap(weights='weight', steps=4).as_clustering())\n",
    "\n",
    "display(pd.DataFrame(rows).set_index('structure'))\n",
    "\n",
    "# save for Parts 2.3 and 3: one membership column per structure\n",
    "pd.DataFrame({'account_id': g_final.vs['name'], **partitions}).to_csv(DATA / 'communities.csv', index=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# plots\n",
    "def community_palette(memb, n_colored=10):\n",
    "    \"\"\"The n_colored largest communities get a colour, in order of size.\"\"\"\n",
    "    top = [c for c, _ in Counter(memb).most_common(n_colored)]\n",
    "    palette = [to_hex(c) for c in plt.get_cmap('tab10').colors]\n",
    "    return {c: palette[i] for i, c in enumerate(top)}\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def community_colors(memb, n_colored=10, other='#d0d0d0'):\n",
    "    pal = community_palette(memb, n_colored)\n",
    "    return [pal.get(m, other) for m in memb]\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "g_draw = g_und.copy()\n",
    "g_draw.es['color'] = [(0.2, 0.2, 0.2, 0.03)] * g_draw.ecount()      # very transparent edges\n",
    "\n",
    "names = list(partitions)\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 3, figsize=(21, 14))\n",
    "for ax, name in zip(axes.ravel(), names):\n",
    "    ig.plot(g_draw, target=ax, layout=layout_final,\n",
    "            vertex_color=community_colors(partitions[name]),\n",
    "            vertex_size=4, vertex_frame_width=0, edge_width=0.3)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'{name}: {len(set(partitions[name]))} communities (10 largest coloured)')\n",
    "    ax.axis('off')\n",
    "for ax in axes.ravel()[len(names):]:\n",
    "    ax.axis('off')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
