    "### Task 2.4\n",
    "\n",
    "Another application of community detection is to identify interesting communities to look at in more detail. In this task we ask you to generate two networks based on the URLs and/or domains posted by the accounts in the original data. Let us call R an entity that can be present in a tweet (R being a specific URL or a top-level domain). A co-action between accounts A and B occurs when A and B post one tweet each at no more than s seconds from each other, with the two tweets both containing the same entity R. A tie between A and B is generated if at least r co-actions have occurred between A and B. Varying the parameters above, generate two networks:\n",
    "\n",
    "- One aimed at identifying bots. For example, using s = one second and R representing URLs. You can assume that it is unlikely for two \"human-driven\" accounts to independently post the same URL (e.g. http://www.lejdd.fr/Politique/Laurent-Fabius-au-JDD-Oui-j-etais-tres-emu-763913) at the same time.\n",
    "- One aimed at identifying ideologically- or topically-related groups of accounts, for example groups of accounts posting the same URL(s) even though not necessarily at the same exact time.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### TIPS & QUESTIONS:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Think of whether you should consider or not retweets to generate this network.\n",
    "- When you choose the filters (the type of entity R, the time interval s, and the number of required co-actions r), try to identify values leading to \"reasonable\" networks: if the resulting network is too dense, it may take too long to analyse it and (most importantly) the network structure may not be usable as a proxy to identify bots and/or ideology.\n",
    "- Use the code you wrote last week, for example the one generating an overview of the network structure, to decide good parameter settings.\n",
    "- Which entities are useful to define the ideology network?\n",
    "\n",
